## Changing the caller's variables

Arguments are copies (lesson 2.02). To let a function change a variable, give it the variable's **address**:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b)
{
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

int main(void)
{
    int x = 1, y = 2;
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("x=%d y=%d\n", x, y);    // x=2 y=1
    return 0;
}
```

The pointers are copies too, but copies of an address still lead to the same variables.

```demo-stack
title: swap works through addresses
call main | main has x and y in its frame.
local x = 1
local y = 2
call swap a=&x b=&y | swap's parameters are addresses pointing back into main's frame.
local temp = 1 | temp = *a reads main's x.
set x = 2 | *a = *b writes into main's frame.
set y = 1 | *b = temp, and the swap is done.
return | swap's frame goes, but main's variables keep the changes.
return 0
```

This is also why `scanf("%d", &n)` needs the `&`: scanf has to store into your variable, so it needs its address.

## Out-parameters

A function returns one value. For more, the caller passes addresses where the extra results go. The return value usually says whether it worked:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

bool divide(int a, int b, int *quotient, int *remainder)
{
    if (b == 0) return false;
    *quotient = a / b;
    *remainder = a % b;
    return true;
}

int main(void)
{
    int q = 0, r = 0;
    if (divide(17, 5, &q, &r)) {
        printf("17 = 5 x %d + %d\n", q, r);
    }
    if (!divide(1, 0, &q, &r)) {
        printf("cannot divide by zero\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The standard library does this everywhere: `strtol(text, &end, 10)` returns the number and stores where it stopped in `end`.

> [!tip] Optional outputs
> Let the caller pass `NULL` for a result it does not want, and check before writing: `if (remainder != NULL) *remainder = a % b;`.

## Pointers to big things

Copying a large structure into a parameter takes time; passing its address costs 8 bytes. `const` (next lesson) says the function only reads through it: `void print_report(const struct report *r)`.

## Never return a local's address

> [!danger] Dangling pointers
> ```c-norun
> int *broken(void)
> {
>     int result = 42;
>     return &result;      // result dies when the function returns
> }
> ```
> The caller gets the address of a stack frame that is already gone. The next function call reuses that memory, and the "42" turns into something else. GCC warns: "function returns address of local variable". A function can return pointers to: the caller's memory (that it was given), static or global variables, string literals, or memory from `malloc` (lesson 4.01).

## Check yourself
```quiz
? Why does scanf need &n rather than n?
- `&` converts the text it reads into a number
- It reads n's old value first and adds to it
+ To store into n, it needs n's address
> Without the address, scanf would only get a copy of n's value.

? swap(int *a, int *b) is called as swap(&x, &y). Inside, what is *a?
- A copy of x
+ x itself
- The address of x
> a is the address; *a is the variable at that address.

? What is wrong with returning the address of a local variable?
- Locals have no address
- Nothing, it is common practice
+ The local stops existing when the function returns
> It leaves a dangling pointer.

? How do functions usually report whether an out-parameter was filled?
- By setting the out-parameter to -1
+ Through their return value, such as true or false
- They cannot report it at all
> Like `divide` above, or scanf's count of items read.
```
